                    ("h2", "Two ways a program can decide"),
                    ("p", "A **rule-based** program follows tests a person wrote: if attendance is below 75, print a warning. The rule is visible. It does not change unless a programmer edits it. A **machine-learning** program is given examples and adjusts internal numbers so that it reproduces the examples and, the designers hope, handles new cases. A spam filter trained on old messages is the second kind. Class XII opens the training process. Here you need the contrast and a few names."),
                    ("ul", [
                        "**Supervised learning** uses examples that already have the right answer, such as 'this picture is a cat'.",
                        "**Unsupervised learning** has no such labels. Clustering students by the pattern of their library use, without being told the 'right' group, is unsupervised.",
                        "**Classification** predicts a category. **Regression** predicts a number, which is the line from Lecture 4.4.",
                        "**Natural language processing** works with human language: translation, or picking the topic of a paragraph.",
                        "**Computer vision** works with images. **Robotics** acts in the physical world.",
                    ]),
                    ("h2", "Stakeholders"),
                    ("p", "A **stakeholder** is a person or group with a real interest in the system. In a system that flags which college applications look 'strong', the stakeholders include the applicants, the admissions staff, the college, and the people who will not apply again if they believe the process is unfair. Their interests are not the same. Staff may want a shorter list. Applicants want a reason they can challenge. A model that is slightly more accurate and completely unexplained serves one of those interests and harms the other."),
                    ("p", "The syllabus asks you to see that culture and values enter the design whether or not anyone mentions them. If the training examples come from one kind of school, the model learns that kind of school. That is not a neutral fact about 'the data'. It is a decision about whose past is being copied. A responsible requirement says who can ask for a human review, what is not allowed as an input (for example a photograph when the decision should not depend on appearance), and how long the application is kept."),
                    ("note", "Example", "Positive uses are real and should be stated without advertising language. A translation tool helps a student read a paper. A vision tool can warn a driver. A timetable tool can save an office a week. The same week you should be able to name the person who is worse off if the tool is wrong, and the appeal they have."),
