                    {"type": "short", "stem": "Contrast supervised and unsupervised learning with one example each.", "answer": "Supervised learning trains on examples that already have answers, such as emails marked spam or not spam. Unsupervised learning looks for groups in data that has no such labels, such as clustering shoppers by what they buy."},
                    {"type": "short", "stem": "An automated short-listing tool is introduced for a scholarship. Name two stakeholders and one conflict between them.", "answer": "Applicants and the selection committee. The committee may want speed. Applicants want to know why they were rejected and to correct a factual mistake. A system with no explanation serves the first interest and blocks the second."},
